.TH "bash\-serverrc" "5" "2026\-02\-01" "bash\-server 0.1.0" "bash\-server file formats"
.SH DESCRIPTION
The \f[B]bash\-serverrc\f[R] file provides configuration directives for
\f[B]bash\-server\f[R](1).
It is read at startup before the server begins accepting connections.
.PP
The default location is \f[B]\(ti/.bash\-serverrc\f[R].
An alternate path may be specified with the \f[B]\(enconfig\f[R]
command\-line option.
.PP
The file is processed line by line in order.
Blank lines and lines whose first non\-whitespace character is
\f[B]#\f[R] are ignored.
For directives that accept a single value, the first matching directive
wins; subsequent duplicates are silently ignored.
.SH FORMAT
Each directive occupies one line and has the form:
.IP
.EX
DIRECTIVE VALUE
.EE
.PP
Leading and trailing whitespace is stripped.
The directive keyword is case\-sensitive.
.SH Directives
.TP
\f[B]socket\f[R] \f[I]PATH\f[R]
Set the Unix domain socket path the server listens on.
\f[I]PATH\f[R] is an absolute filesystem path.
The parent directory must exist and be writable by the server process.
.RS
If this directive is absent, the socket path is resolved through the
following fallback chain (first match wins):
.IP "1." 3
\f[B]\(ensocket\f[R] command\-line option
.IP "2." 3
\f[B]$BASH_SERVER_SOCKET\f[R] environment variable
.IP "3." 3
\f[B]socket\f[R] directive in \f[B]\(ti/.bash\-serverrc\f[R]
.IP "4." 3
\f[B]$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/bash\-server/sock\f[R]
.IP "5." 3
\f[B]/tmp/bash\-server\-\f[R]\f[I]<uid>\f[R]\f[B]/sock\f[R]
.RE
.PP
Future versions may introduce additional directives.
Unrecognized directives produce a warning on stderr but do not prevent
startup.
.SH EXAMPLES
Minimal configuration setting only the socket path:
.IP
.EX
# \(ti/.bash\-serverrc \(em bash\-server configuration
socket /run/user/1000/bash\-server/sock
.EE
.PP
Configuration with comments and blank lines:
.IP
.EX
# bash\-server config
# Written 2025\-04\-10

# Listen on a custom socket path
socket /home/alice/.local/bash\-server/sock

# Future directives will go here
.EE
.PP
Because first\-match wins, only the first \f[B]socket\f[R] line takes
effect:
.IP
.EX
socket /tmp/primary.sock
socket /tmp/secondary.sock    # ignored
.EE
.SH FILES
.TP
\f[B]\(ti/.bash\-serverrc\f[R]
Default configuration file location.
